# **Assignment Two – Peer Observation**

The new model (institutional audit) firmly places the responsibilities on institutions themselves to have robust internal systems for assuring quality and standards. (DfES, 2003, para. 4.15)

Peer observation is a vehicle of improving teaching methods via obtaining feedback from colleagues. There is clear evidence that teachers find peer-based review useful (Kohut et al, 2007, among others), but managerial quality control kind of approach seems to be destructive to teaching peer review efficiency (Lam 2001 provides an excellent example of teachers disliking the peer reviewing process mostly because of the administrative pressure).

Peer review in teaching is commonplace in the UK, not always voluntarily (Shorthand (2004)). It is so widespread that it is a default requirement in this university for lecturer probation, as well as a requirement for the teaching certificate course. Therefore, I arranged two lectures, one where I observed my colleague, and another where a colleague observed me.

I studied in the US, and US culture of teaching is somewhat different. Only 37% of research Economics departments engage in peer review (Becker and Watts 1999), and the weight put on peer review in career decisions is not too large (zero, according to many of current faculty of other universities that I know). This experience of peer observation, imposed on me by the teaching certificate course, therefore allowed me to engage in a novel activity, and I learned a lot from it.

**Before Observing**

Peer observation is usually classified in to three models based upon their aims and objectives: *evaluation model*, needed for evaluating the performance of teachers that affects their careers; *developmental model*, where peers attempt to help their colleague improve his skills and tactics; and *peer-review model*, where both observer and observant reflect upon their teaching experience (Gosling, 2005; Siddiqui et al, 2007; Gosling, 2011). These are important to distinguish because, before one engages in the evaluation, one needs to think about what kind of questions one expects to answer with this observation.

When planning my observation exercises, I first had to choose the proforma to use. I decided to learn as much as I can about myself, and for that, I used the standard proforma (Appendix 1) since its questions do not limit those who supervise much. My colleagues had nothing against this forma, so this is the one we ended up using at the end of the day.

Institutionally, academics are somewhat diffident to having more centralized activities (Lomas and Nicholls 2005 provide an excellent case study), and the School only recently started doing the peer review of teaching. This was implemented from the University center, changing the freshly hired Lecturers’ contracts unilaterally, raising discomfort and imposing additional time costs on faculty whose research time is quite scarce already. However, it was easy for me to find a senior faculty member to conduct a peer review of teaching (simultaneously fulfilling my confirmation requirement), and my colleague whom I observed was quite enthusiastic about the experience. We agreed that our purpose is to improve learning for the students, not to find flaws.

## **Me, The Observed**

My colleague, a senior faculty member of significant UK experience, came over to my lecture in October. He came over, and stayed from the beginning to the end. Afterwards, we’ve discussed my performance in the nearby park, and then he emailed me the typed-up form.

The class was an economics lecture for level 3 students, required for Econ students, optional for Business and some other students. The class is usually well-attended, about 80% of enrolled show up. In the beginning, I set up my camera (I tried to videotape my lectures, to review later), and I spent some time talking about comments that I collected anonymously online about my approach to teaching. This allowed me to demonstrate that some things, while creating inconvenience, are a result of a thoroughly deliberated decision aimed at improving the educational experience. This also allowed me to provide something to do for people who came on time, while not depriving people who were reasonably late of their lecture time. Then I start the “Previous Class” part: I reminded what happened in the last lecture. This helps me to warm up my English, and students remember the terminology, as well as get a decent idea about where exactly to add this particular lecture in their mind map. Finally, I started my lecture, conducted in the way that I expected, and closed with an overview of how what we learned today and recently is different from the previous large topic, and what should the students expect next time.

What did I learned: The observer was surprised that I was assigned a large classroom for a small audience, but noted in his observation form that I handled this well: students were engaged, and answered questions  reasonably eagerly. He went to great distances underlining good practices that I am following, underlined that I am clearly competent, and so he sees no problems to smooth out with me. Some of the strategies I used were recognized (the warmup) and appreciated.

Whereas I knew that I am unlikely to have any large issues (I’ve been teaching for 4 years now, not counting being a TA in the US), I learned a few things. One about the teaching culture in the School: the priorities are the quality of lecture material and student engagement. Pretty much every comment that my observer made was about either one or another. Second: having an observer in the audience is not terribly distracting, and students are not too intimidated by his presence. Finally: peers are open to assist with observing teaching, they are open to helping out a fellow faculty member, and they see value in peer assisted teaching. Third: the observer shared his tactic of teaching a certain recurring mechanism in Economics. At some point, the lecturer needs to illustrate where do certain lines move. This can be done in different ways. Usual PDF slides only allow the lecturer to pre-plan the movements, and this makes the lecture very rigid: answering questions of students cannot have the same eloquence. Drawing graphs with chalk or markers quickly becomes messy, and fumbled constant erasing does not help. What my observer does is that he uses the transparency projector on top of the computer projector, and the moving graphs are moved by hand on the transparency projector. This is an interesting trick, and I will try to use it next time.

Reflection: I suppose I should have been more clear with my observer about what I want to learn from my experience. The questions in his head were about whether I am the right person for the job. I guess having a form somewhat limited his thinking, and I am not sure if he really needed to learn anything from me. A peer from my teaching certificate course might have done a better peer-review aimed job, my observer was more aiming for the developmental angle. From my point of view, I might not have lost a lot, I still got a lot of valuable feedback. I cannot make assumptions about how my colleague would benefit if he tried to learn from me, whether he needed to learn from me, et cetera, but I know I still have a lot to learn. I used this experience to frame my observing more towards peer review-based observation.

## **Me, The Observer**

I was observing a colleague of a similar experience and of a similar background (US training, non-native English speaker) who was teaching a class in Economics, but in a different subfield. I attended his afternoon lecture, together with students.

The lecturer came in on time sharp, spend 5 minutes setting up the computer and the projector, and was able to start as prescribed by the university recommendations. Then the lecturer spent 5 to 10 minutes reiterating on what was in the last lecture. This followed with a lecture on the new material, with intermissions filled with questions to the audience, chalkboard exercises, and examples. I filled up the proforma (see Appendix 1) as the lecture was progressing, so that I can give comments afterwards. Omitting comments on my colleague’s teaching style, I will outline the lessons I’ve derived from my observation experience.

What did I learned: It was unusual to see some of your own mannerisms in other people’s presentations. I videotaped myself a few times before. There are some practices that I intentionally got rid of, and I saw some of them, and I congratulated myself of getting rid of them. For instance, I caught myself a couple of years ago saying “Okay?” It was very annoying, but that’s not the thing one would tell to a colleague. I stopped doing this by making myself keep silence instead of uttering the unnecessary. Moreover, this “Okay?” became more powerful: students started confirming when I really needed an answer from them instead of taking it as a sort of a phonetic ellipsis…

Another good practice that will help my colleague that I came up with in my career was to make the introduction and the conclusion part to stand out. By having 2-3 minutes of introduction, I warm up my English speaking skills, students remember what we did before (if we continue the topic that we had in the previous lecture) or connect to the course’s objectives (if we start a new topic), and I let people who are a bit late to still catch up with the lecture without everyone else feeling they are wasting their time. Having a conclusion makes it clear for students that we’re done for today, that there’s a couple of minutes when they can ask questions, and I can repeat again the main points of the lecture to be sure that everyone got them. If someone missed a point, we still have a couple of minutes after the lecture to discuss it one-to-one.

Finally, asking questions from the audience. This is a good practice to be sure that students are on board, to create a refreshing break in the lecture, and to stimulate some reflection on new material. This was used by my colleague with mixed success: some questions were quickly answered by a student in the audience, while some other questions were met with uncomfortable silence. I underestimated how intimidating people look when they expect an answer in total silence; I however did know that it is somewhat uncomfortable, so I tried to break this silence in 5-10 seconds after it’s clear that the answer is not going out. I guess my point here is that students are people, and when they are not in the position of power (like during the lecture), they would not be able to ask a clarifying question. It is not easy to ask an easy question!

The big mistake that he did at the end of the class was that he let out his frustration with the lack of students’ preparation at home. Apparently, he expected students to read a chapter from a textbook, and he based the lecture around that chapter, and the lack of preparation by students pretty much undermined all his own preparations. I am saying that this is a mistake because students perceived the negativity that he emanated much earlier than the content of his message, which made them less receptive to his message. The way I would have dealt with this is I’d remove the slides and everything else I’ve prepared for the next class, and I’d spend this class talking about what they were supposed to learn by themselves. Students would see that the lack of preparation at home translates to less knowledge received. If I were extra angry, I’d have an additional class scheduled. However, showing negative emotions, like in any other relationship, never improves students’ motivation.

Talking afterwards: I have delivered my advice to my colleague. He seemed nervous before the discussion, but as we talked, he relaxed a bit, and explained his behaviour, which helped me to see the above lessons clearer. I think much of what I’ve suggested does not seem as very important to a US-trained economist, students in US pay significantly more for their education to have time to be sensitive about the lecture delivery. I think when my colleague realized that my point is to suggest easy ways to improve the perception of him in the student body and therefore in the department, he started to react a bit more openly. I found it beneficial to talk afterwards because some of the points I’ve raised above only became lucid after I discussed minor things first, and got my colleague’s input on other things.

Summing up: it pays off a lot to observe someone of the same background while in the audience to sense better the audience’s reaction to actions and mannerisms, not only to help the observed to weed out the inefficient practices, but also to look for the same practices in self. Very likely, same background left the same imprint in behaviour. I helped my colleague to be a better teacher, I helped his students to learn better, I helped my department to be a more efficient environment.

## **Peer Observation as a Method to Obtain Feedback**

After experiencing both sides of peer review, I have to say I found more benefit than I expected. Most of the faculty is smart enough to not make big mistakes (come unprepared, mock students, lose homeworks, etc), so peer review seems unnecessary: big things are probably handled well, small things are probably unimportant, so why bother?

The improvement is subtle, yet significant. We, the faculty, develop our own practices, our own tricks and strategies to teach students better. The natural force of evolution will make sure that those who are better able to teach---whether with their innate ability or as a result of (self)training via reflection and reading relevant literature---will be teaching more students, will attract better applicants, and in general will be rewarded for their superior teaching skills. So, those seek rewards for their teaching skills, will be able to improve them without peer review. This model makes sense, but it neglects the fact that the competition is not between individual faculty members, but between university bodies. Our School competes with Schools of other universities. It makes sense to engage in teaching your colleagues your tricks and strategies, because this will allow good practices to propagate faster in the department, making it more attractive for able students.

I found that having a senior faculty member tell you that you are fine creates assurance, and it is valuable in itself (even if the lecturer was suspecting that he was fine at the very beginning). I found that faculty cares enough to get the teaching materials in advance of the lecture, and makes sure the supply is adequate. I found that the priorities about teaching in the School are centered not so much at students’ evaluations, but more on students’ engagement, sufficient supply of study materials, and clarity of delivery.

Faculty does not perceive teaching in a similar fashion as, for instance, food service people perceive their clientele. The relationship of the faculty member and the student is much longer and much more codependent than what’s usually happening in the business world. This is why peer review in teaching is promoted by its proponents. We might disagree about the students’ opinions about our research, but if there is someone competent about how one should teach in this specific environment, it’s the other people who teach in the same environment.

As any good thing, especially involving more than one person, peer observation should happen with due preparation.  The observed and the observer must agree upon what is their plan before they do the observation: this will allow the observer to carry out a better observation, providing better service to the observed. Both good and bad practices must be noticed: even if both participants know that they are present, a discussion of the issue might provide a solution. Finally, faculty members are much more open to observe this than people usually expect, and it never hurts to ask!

